  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 140 AM EDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\n             -----------Synoptic Overview-----------\n\nFlorida remains beneath a weakness between a pair of mid-level\nanticyclones early this morning. Near-normal H5 heights reside over\nthe state, but subtle vorticity perturbations continue to meander\nnearby. Total moisture values have returned to near normal over \nthe peninsula. Little change is expected in the overall pattern \nthrough the remainder of the work week. The trough over the \nnortheast Gulf is expected to very slowly retrograde and fill, \nallowing height rises across Florida. This will ensure continued \nsouth to southwesterly flow above H85 through at least Friday. At \nthe surface, an axis of high pressure will attempt to move \nnorthward, holding close to our latitude through Friday as well.\n\nEnsemble guidance remains in strong agreement into the extended\nforecast. The members bridge the mid-level highs over the Southern\nU.S. and Gulf by the weekend and into early next week, allowing\nsurface high pressure to lift north of Florida. Near- to slightly\nabove-normal moisture should remain in place, but steering flow\nfor convection should turn onshore and deepen, lasting well into \nnext week.\n\nThe National Hurricane Center gives a tropical wave in the Atlantic\nMDR (Main Development Region) a 70% chance of tropical cyclone\nformation over the next couple of days. While it is far too early\nto know whether this feature will affect the U.S., it's a reminder\nto be prepared as peak hurricane season is upon us.\n\n             -------Sensible Weather & Impacts-------\n\nNow - Tonight...\n\nA handful of storms have lasted well into the night, with frequent\nlightning and brief gusty winds affecting parts of Brevard\nCounty. This activity should dissipate late in the overnight,\nleaving us with a quiet start to the day. Hi-res guidance then\nsuggests that a few storms will attempt to form by mid-afternoon\nalong the I-95 corridor as the sea breeze moves inland. Additional\nstorms are likely to flare up along boundary collisions with the\nGulf breeze over the interior by early evening. With ample moisture\nand weak energy aloft, there is little reason to doubt high overall\ncoverage later today (50-60% coast, 60-70% interior).\n\nAs in recent days, the primary threats remain frequent lightning,\nwind gusts of 45 to 55 mph, and torrential rainfall. HREF members\nshow a 10% chance of rainfall amounts reaching 3 inches today, \nespecially over the interior. If this occurs over urban areas, \nlocalized flooding may result.\n\nWidespread low to mid 90s once again today, with max heat indices\n100-105.\n\nThursday - Friday...\n\nNo major changes to the expected weather setup as we wrap the work\nweek. Light surface winds will allow for a fairly robust sea breeze\ncirculation, while moderate south-southwest flow persists above\n5,000 ft. This portends daily rounds of showers and storms with\nfairly high coverage (50-70%) in the afternoon and evening. Storm\nthreats will be similar to what is expected later today. With the\nlight offshore steering flow, a few storms may push back toward\nthe coast, especially on Thursday evening. Beach-goers should\nmonitor the western sky and seek sturdy shelter if you hear thunder.\n\nExpect seasonably hot and humid conditions before the storms form\nboth days.\n\nWeekend - Middle Of Next Week...\n\nAs high pressure builds and gains latitude, onshore flow will\nbecome established through the lowest 10,000 feet. Scattered storms\nshould form each day on the advancing sea breeze, with an eventual\ncollision with the Gulf breeze either near or west of Orlando and\nthe Kissimmee Basin by evening. Beachside locations will have the\nbest chance of staying dry, while some interior locales will likely\nsee a storm each afternoon. Some modulation may occur from day to\nday due to moisture fluctuations in the onshore flow regime. Expect\nno substantial change to our seasonable heat and humidity as we\nround out August.",
